#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124271 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abbdd778-7ace-5539-9d6f-cdf01804b237" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Stochastic Matrix Given Some Data"}] :question/id #uuid "e8f30d47-bfd2-50ea-b2ad-d474e2a58d29" :question/math-academy-id "e-5598" :question/problem "There are two groups of people in a particular city: those who use their own vehicles and those who use public transport. A study has shown that, each year\n\n- $35\\%$ of people who used their own vehicles swap to public transport, and\n\n- $25\\%$ of people who used public transport swap to their own vehicles.\n\nGive the stochastic matrix that describes how people change their means of transportation each year." :question/worked-solution "We are given the following situation for each year: \n\n- $35\\%$ of people who used their own vehicles swap to public transport. This implies that $65\\%$ of them remain using their own vehicles.\n\n- $25\\%$ of people who used public transport swap to their own vehicles. This implies that $75\\%$ of them remain using public transport.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Vehicles** | **Public Transport ** |\n| To: | **Vehicles** | $0.65$ | $0.25$ |\n| **Public Transport** | $0.35$ | $0.75$ |  |\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/assets/b21fee1abe56dcfdc687be14278c62a20d201ff00e96f1e5918ce22d9dc8b03c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124272 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e93dde4-a8e8-548a-801e-3d9a320a4513" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Stochastic Matrix and Making a Prediction"}] :question/id #uuid "95a45716-8c29-5955-af3c-43b6344912da" :question/math-academy-id "e-5599" :question/problem "A certain industry produces $2$ types of sugar, namely sugar $A$ and sugar $B$. The following table describes how the number of consumers changes between both types of sugar in a month:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Sugar $A$** | **Sugar $B$** |\n| To: | **Sugar $A$** | $0.3$ | $0.45$ |\n| **Sugar $B$** | $0.7$ | $0.55$ |  |\n\nThat is, $70\\%$ of sugar $A$ customers switch to sugar $B$ each month, and $45\\%$ of sugar $B$ customers switch to sugar $A.$\n\nCurrently, $600$ customers use sugar $A,$ while $400$ customers use sugar $B.$ How many customers will use sugar $A$ in one month? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." :question/worked-solution "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/assets/451b82e8cb00ede7b0ddf73f35c0555d0eeeeefc5beb5ce4fddd54f0df5f2577.png)\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of sugar is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/assets/56952aeed1d718033af8a3e6d09e8e269f1d70aa8dc31609b8289f025f2e6bd6.png)\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/assets/cae1bed6b8b01b3ff66c2e8bacf7a10f6c0ece53f3aeac0fb1a51e8fc3c07e8e.png)\n\nTo find the state vector $\\mathbf{x}_1$ after one month, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_1=P\\mathbf{x}_0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.3 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.55\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.6 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.36 \\\\ 0.64\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one month, $\\color{blue}36\\%$ of customers will use sugar $A.$ Computing $36\\%$ of $1\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000 \\cdot 0.36 = 360.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $360$ customers will use sugar $A$ in one month."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124281 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4821de45-1b6b-5b4f-be0e-96cf5395bc8d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a State Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "09540ff7-0444-5169-b582-471be2f1972d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5971" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/assets/165917feacd28344502759fc6e03f96c5b54d9530701916b746fc489374a6d0b.png) \n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector $\\mathbf{x}_0$ given above. Calculate the state vector $\\mathbf{x}_2.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the state vector $\\mathbf{x}_2,$ we apply two steps of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x_1}=P\\mathbf{x}_0, \\qquad \\mathbf{x}_2=P\\mathbf{x}_1\n$$\n\nAfter the first step, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.18 \\\\ 0.82\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, after the second step, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.18 \\\\ 0.82\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.172 \\\\ 0.828\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124343 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66b24a98-c641-5e08-8bec-30f221d1bb22" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Stochastic Vectors and Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/id #uuid "25622a0e-3e5b-5f09-bef6-afaa30d153d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-9162" :question/problem "Which of the following are stochastic matrices?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/assets/70ee929c71d89a443d3ea0efc39ed225f344685059b86a04285a118a476da0f8.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$ A matrix is stochastic if all its columns are stochastic vectors.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices in turn.\n\n- Matrix $\\bf{U}$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its first column is not a stochastic vector (it contains negative entries): \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 0 \\\\ 1.2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Matrix $\\bf{V}$ is a stochastic matrix. Indeed, each column is a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries are nonnegative and add up to one): \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}0.35 & 0.95 \\\\ 0.65 & 0.05\\end{aligned}]\\,\\begin{aligned}0.35+0.65=1 \\\\ 0.95+0.05=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Matrix $\\bf{W}$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its third column is not a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries don't add up to one): \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 0 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.5 & 0 & 0.7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\bf{V}$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124686 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66b24a98-c641-5e08-8bec-30f221d1bb22" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Stochastic Vectors and Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa30e03b-73c6-58a8-8ca2-d33652dd55f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121136} {:answer/id #uuid "345be07d-f682-5e56-9287-b3b6e80a5244" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$u$ and $w$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121137} {:answer/id #uuid "40c12b32-1184-5eef-88e6-3794bd5165c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$u$, $v$, and $w$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121138} {:answer/id #uuid "76af5fa2-a466-5fc8-bf49-da98e388d9a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$u$ and $v$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121139} {:answer/id #uuid "7e6750c6-50ee-5544-af49-77996fcd6cdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$u$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e6750c6-50ee-5544-af49-77996fcd6cdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$u$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121140} :answer-field/id #uuid "c728a98d-78c3-51e5-8097-83a2c3ff278a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d6371d4-1a60-5526-b7b1-69a38ead3549" :question/math-academy-id "q-109881" :question/problem "Which of the following are stochastic vectors?\n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0.5\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-0.45 \\\\ 1.55\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our vectors in turn.\n\n- Vector $u$ is a stochastic vector. Indeed, \n\n- its entries are all nonnegative, and\n\n- the entries add up to one:\n\n$$\n0.5+0+0.5=1\n$$\n\n- Vector $v$ is *not* a stochastic vector since it contains negative entries.\n\n- Vector $w$ is *not* a stochastic vector since the entries do not add up to one:\n\n$$\n0+0=0\\ne 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$u$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339154 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4821de45-1b6b-5b4f-be0e-96cf5395bc8d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a State Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54537149-644d-55ea-8a71-67a4b31beaec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.28 \\\\ 0.32 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339156} {:answer/id #uuid "bc7583a4-a122-5d76-98f6-f23eafa3fa27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.29 \\\\ 0.43 \\\\ 0.28\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339157} {:answer/id #uuid "228e541d-0aa0-55a7-9488-46e28ef8acd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.25 \\\\ 0.36 \\\\ 0.39\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339158} {:answer/id #uuid "1818f04a-0e79-588d-98ef-1cfd66c7c244" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.3 \\\\ 0.42 \\\\ 0.28\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339159} {:answer/id #uuid "823c5c89-0063-5931-9007-1ef2eb3b4efb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.32 \\\\ 0.27 \\\\ 0.41\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc7583a4-a122-5d76-98f6-f23eafa3fa27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.29 \\\\ 0.43 \\\\ 0.28\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339157} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ae5581c-d639-5ad7-bd71-92db4460a04c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0caf7fb-7634-5f04-a5b1-3f3c94101c66" :question/math-academy-id "q-135442" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.3 & 0.2 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.6 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.4\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 0.5 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector ${x}_{0}$ given above. Calculate the state vector ${x}_{1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the state vector ${x}_{1},$ we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.3 & 0.2 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.6 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 0.5 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.29 \\\\ 0.43 \\\\ 0.28\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339161 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4821de45-1b6b-5b4f-be0e-96cf5395bc8d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a State Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecc288e9-9f26-5bf2-b5d4-03fbb76e8de8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.27 \\\\ 0.73\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339163} {:answer/id #uuid "d01f503c-4cf4-5ffb-a87b-a4bd71610170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.17 \\\\ 0.83\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339164} {:answer/id #uuid "4a0bbb9d-c64f-5d21-aa47-7f3f2e61237f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.21 \\\\ 0.79\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339165} {:answer/id #uuid "4692d91b-d06a-58c5-9403-ed111cf51359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.32 \\\\ 0.68\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339166} {:answer/id #uuid "2f64efc5-6550-59d6-90b3-26ce788f5f3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.23 \\\\ 0.77\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d01f503c-4cf4-5ffb-a87b-a4bd71610170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.17 \\\\ 0.83\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339164} :answer-field/id #uuid "a34607bd-5177-5379-acb8-0df34fc06140" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "110594bb-a5fc-55c7-92d8-2e98a70f7012" :question/math-academy-id "q-135447" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.8\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0.3 \\\\ 0.7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector ${x}_{0}$ given above. Calculate the state vector ${x}_{1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the state vector ${x}_{1},$ we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.3 \\\\ 0.7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.17 \\\\ 0.83\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4821de45-1b6b-5b4f-be0e-96cf5395bc8d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a State Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93012f05-6416-511b-b5f4-05767a8f7dad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.312 \\\\ 0.688\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339170} {:answer/id #uuid "6f9ebbd8-3581-55bc-a6a2-1601eb5fe0d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.324 \\\\ 0.676\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339171} {:answer/id #uuid "7301decc-e4cb-5420-b963-05c7c9305215" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.434 \\\\ 0.566\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339172} {:answer/id #uuid "d8505ec2-14fa-5c0a-81e2-7f7c097f6c59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.364 \\\\ 0.636\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339173} {:answer/id #uuid "fb79c0ad-eae2-57e6-a5e0-02d3f28927ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.296 \\\\ 0.704\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f9ebbd8-3581-55bc-a6a2-1601eb5fe0d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.324 \\\\ 0.676\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339171} :answer-field/id #uuid "12f641fe-2871-5149-bfe0-0cb7ec41d57c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b6f2bb21-8c66-5d1d-89c2-970e35eeb5ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-135450" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.6\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 0.9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector ${x}_{0}$ given above. Calculate the state vector ${x}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the state vector ${x}_{2},$ we apply two steps of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0},\\,{x}_{2}=P{x}_{1}\n$$\n\nAfter the first step, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 0.9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.38 \\\\ 0.62\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, after the second step, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.38 \\\\ 0.62\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.324 \\\\ 0.676\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339175 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e93dde4-a8e8-548a-801e-3d9a320a4513" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Stochastic Matrix and Making a Prediction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98ec2983-2653-562b-a96e-863ff082b2da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339177} {:answer/id #uuid "c780875d-94ba-5678-9842-a32cfbce6f6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339178} {:answer/id #uuid "e8b75824-49e3-5879-99a4-1b2847a7cc79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339179} {:answer/id #uuid "00566601-c5f6-5ca2-9cf6-d95445c8aa2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339180} {:answer/id #uuid "f5fab232-a7bc-5b75-a91b-ec8e0cd8a3ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5fab232-a7bc-5b75-a91b-ec8e0cd8a3ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339181} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4d86fbd-2ba8-5a9e-af75-a6c013906351" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3bc34242-e133-5fba-9823-018a7dbf9dab" :question/math-academy-id "q-135458" :question/problem "A certain company produces two types of motorcycles: classic and high-cylinder. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between both types of motorcycles each decade:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Classic** | **High-Cylinder** |\n| To: | **Classic** | $0.6$ | $0.2$ |\n| **High-Cylinder** | $0.4$ | $0.8$ |  |\n\nThat is, $40%$ of classic motorcycle customers switch to high-cylinder each decade, and $20%$ of high-cylinder motorcycle customers switch to Classic.\n\nCurrently, $7\\,000$ customers use classic motorcycles while $3\\,000$ customers use high-cylinder motorcycles. How many customers will use high-cylinder motorcycles in one decade? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." :question/worked-solution "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.6 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of motorcycle is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}7\\,000 \\\\ 3\\,000\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(10\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{10\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}7\\,000 \\\\ 3\\,000\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one decade, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.6 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.48 \\\\ 0.52\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one decade, $52%$ of customers will use high-cylinder motorcycles. Computing $52%$ of $10\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n10\\,000\\cdot 0.52=5\\,200.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5\\,200$ customers will use high-cylinder motorcycles in one decade."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e93dde4-a8e8-548a-801e-3d9a320a4513" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Stochastic Matrix and Making a Prediction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70b19f85-8219-5259-a797-6b720a7677fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "320\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339184} {:answer/id #uuid "f526a866-01da-5244-9b31-58ba7afe55ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "380\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339185} {:answer/id #uuid "090808fe-f145-5375-bcba-cebf519f84ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "430\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339186} {:answer/id #uuid "1b7d8e75-ef95-5123-8368-23d83857a25a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "280\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339187} {:answer/id #uuid "1466fc98-9779-59f9-9c03-f5c57361f58f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1466fc98-9779-59f9-9c03-f5c57361f58f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339188} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f548f38-d37c-5a8d-8446-19cedf6297ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c83a21c4-20b5-5d81-82df-2c0054d8944c" :question/math-academy-id "q-135460" :question/problem "A certain company produces three types of milk: whole, skim, and lactose-free. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between the three types of milk each year:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Whole** | **Skim** | **Lactose-free** |\n| To: | **Whole** | $0.5$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |\n| **Skim** | $0.3$ | $0.4$ | $0.4$ |  |\n| **Lactose-free** | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |  |\n\nFor example, $30%$ of whole milk customers switch to skim milk each year, and $20%$ of lactose-free milk customers switch to whole milk.\n\nCurrently, $400\\,000$ customers consume whole milk, $400\\,000$ customers consume skim milk, and $200\\,000$ customers consume lactose-free milk. How many customers will consume whole milk after one year? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." :question/worked-solution "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.3 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3 & 0.4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of milk is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}400\\,000 \\\\ 400\\,000 \\\\ 200\\,000\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}400\\,000 \\\\ 400\\,000 \\\\ 200\\,000\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 \\\\ 0.4 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.3 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3 & 0.4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.4 \\\\ 0.4 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.36 \\\\ 0.36 \\\\ 0.28\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one year, $36%$ of customers will consume whole milk. Computing $36%$ of $1\\,000\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.36=360\\,000.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $360\\,000$ customers will consume whole milk after one year."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e93dde4-a8e8-548a-801e-3d9a320a4513" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Stochastic Matrix and Making a Prediction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c802ab6-a774-55a2-ba3a-78dca2c87b59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "340\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339191} {:answer/id #uuid "f837d30e-5eb2-51d0-a063-30752cd48247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "240\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339192} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ffd6c5-f462-513c-a984-0b636995dc1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "470\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339193} {:answer/id #uuid "38ca66e9-278f-535f-8e35-8f246237a5ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "390\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339194} {:answer/id #uuid "3a881244-9a4d-5fb4-acdf-d84267e255c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "290\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a881244-9a4d-5fb4-acdf-d84267e255c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "290\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339195} :answer-field/id #uuid "237c83fd-10f6-5c94-9c97-1a878eb8b293" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "04b428ea-6569-5051-b764-669659243c1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-135465" :question/problem "A certain company produces three types of ground coffee: fine, medium, and coarse. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between the three types of ground coffee each year:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Fine** | **Medium** | **Coarse** |\n| To: | **Fine** | $0.5$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ |\n| **Medium** | $0.3$ | $0.6$ | $0.4$ |  |\n| **Coarse** | $0.2$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |  |\n\nFor example, $30%$ of fine ground coffee customers switch to medium ground coffee each year, and $20%$ of coarse ground coffee customers switch to fine ground coffee.\n\nCurrently, $300\\,000$ customers buy fine ground coffee, $500\\,000$ customers buy medium ground coffee, and $200\\,000$ customers buy coarse ground coffee. How many customers will purchase fine ground coffee in one year? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." :question/worked-solution "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.2 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.6 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of ground coffee is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}300\\,000 \\\\ 500\\,000 \\\\ 200\\,000\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}300\\,000 \\\\ 500\\,000 \\\\ 200\\,000\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.3 \\\\ 0.5 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.2 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.6 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.3 \\\\ 0.5 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.29 \\\\ 0.47 \\\\ 0.24\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one year, $29%$ of customers will buy fine ground coffee. Computing $29%$ of $1\\,000\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.29=290\\,000.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $290\\,000$ customers will buy fine ground coffee in one year."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e93dde4-a8e8-548a-801e-3d9a320a4513" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Stochastic Matrix and Making a Prediction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57f41175-8631-53ca-905d-1aa815f840bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "290" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339198} {:answer/id #uuid "8e53e619-5d0d-5c44-80e7-cc6eb47f044a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "420" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339199} {:answer/id #uuid "86f475a4-9810-5305-a243-fbd7527ce134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "330" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339200} {:answer/id #uuid "d0d6ac7b-eeb0-54b6-b3aa-2f177bb4c9f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "310" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339201} {:answer/id #uuid "12f265fc-3789-5bc9-8fce-070d1d0bbf98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86f475a4-9810-5305-a243-fbd7527ce134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "330" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339200} :answer-field/id #uuid "d09405d1-0c7d-5393-affa-682c82829d3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "675dc848-9bb3-55cc-acc9-5f6cc9b0d9c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-135466" :question/problem "A certain cinema sells three types of popcorn: traditional, butter, and caramelized. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between the three types of popcorn each month:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Traditional** | **Butter** | **Caramelized** |\n| To: | **Traditional** | $0.4$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n| **Butter** | $0.3$ | $0.5$ | $0.2$ |  |\n| **Caramelized** | $0.3$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ |  |\n\nFor example, $30%$ of traditional popcorn customers switch to butter popcorn each month, and $40%$ of butter popcorn customers switch to caramelized popcorn.\n\nCurrently, $400$ customers buy traditional popcorn, $300$ customers buy butter popcorn, and $300$ customers buy caramelized popcorn. How many customers will purchase butter popcorn in one month? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." :question/worked-solution "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.5 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of popcorn customers is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}400 \\\\ 300 \\\\ 300\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}400 \\\\ 300 \\\\ 300\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 \\\\ 0.3 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.5 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.4 \\\\ 0.3 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.25 \\\\ 0.33 \\\\ 0.42\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one year, $33%$ of customers will buy butter popcorn. Computing $33%$ of $1\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\cdot 0.33=330.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $330$ customers will buy butter popcorn in one month."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66b24a98-c641-5e08-8bec-30f221d1bb22" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Stochastic Vectors and Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16e613e2-6d0d-53e5-8657-3d907d9bb5bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$u,$ $v,$ and $w$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339205} {:answer/id #uuid "7ad5be55-e33a-5b7b-9029-ec64d9c62fca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$w$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339206} {:answer/id #uuid "47032687-3142-5650-bf96-1338798b0125" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$u$ and $v$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339207} {:answer/id #uuid "0a65d192-66ac-5803-b9f7-0def781477b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339208} {:answer/id #uuid "fda39a29-456e-59be-afe1-60d438978098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ and $w$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fda39a29-456e-59be-afe1-60d438978098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ and $w$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339209} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc5de049-629b-5b07-8fdc-67c03529f585" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60493d3a-a1d2-50c0-a800-38f0bca06ee2" :question/math-academy-id "q-135483" :question/problem "Which of the following are stochastic vectors?\n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}0.45 \\\\ 0.55 \\\\ -0.05\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0.65 \\\\ 0.15 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our vectors in turn.\n\n- Vector $u$ is *not* a stochastic vector since it contains negative entries.\n\n- Vector $v$ is a stochastic vector. Indeed, \n\n- its entries are all nonnegative, and\n\n- the entries add up to one:\n\n$$\n0.7+0.3=1\n$$\n\n- Vector $w$ is a stochastic vector. Indeed, \n\n- its entries are all nonnegative, and\n\n- the entries add up to one:\n\n$$\n0.65+0.15+0.2=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$v$ and $w$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66b24a98-c641-5e08-8bec-30f221d1bb22" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Stochastic Vectors and Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fea52d41-5f99-5f73-85a7-28c75cbe32cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$u,$ $v,$ and $w$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339212} {:answer/id #uuid "8a091220-b8c1-55f9-b9e5-18ebec307d98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$w$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339213} {:answer/id #uuid "548da9a8-23f4-542c-99d7-893c1ec4e12c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$u$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339214} {:answer/id #uuid "6192a61a-0edd-5e31-bf74-8114ac1e6682" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$u$ and $v$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339215} {:answer/id #uuid "e47f9a8f-2f23-5e60-bbdf-e021d70b1529" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$u$ and $w$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e47f9a8f-2f23-5e60-bbdf-e021d70b1529" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$u$ and $w$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339216} :answer-field/id #uuid "561a31d8-e775-5904-8b22-46ef410361bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "10395110-66fb-5363-ba85-098bdc5e23da" :question/math-academy-id "q-135484" :question/problem "Which of the following are stochastic vectors?\n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}0.15 \\\\ 0.85\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 \\\\ -0.1 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 0.3 \\\\ 0.5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our vectors in turn.\n\n- Vector $u$ is a stochastic vector. Indeed, \n\n- its entries are all nonnegative, and\n\n- the entries add up to one:\n\n$$\n0.15+0.85=1\n$$\n\n- Vector $v$ is *not* a stochastic vector since it contains negative entries.\n\n- Vector $w$ is a stochastic vector. Indeed, \n\n- its entries are all nonnegative, and\n\n- the entries add up to one:\n\n$$\n0.2+0.3+0.5=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$u$ and $w$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66b24a98-c641-5e08-8bec-30f221d1bb22" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Stochastic Vectors and Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba3c5034-e00e-53e8-b4fa-e87a1bcf2fc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$w$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339219} {:answer/id #uuid "6afb012e-f43b-5f26-b7e5-bcaddcd5f573" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339220} {:answer/id #uuid "75e0b4e9-b4ba-5fa0-8c01-e169bc1d1c56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$u$ and $v$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339221} {:answer/id #uuid "deed4926-2735-5948-a9f2-a013ce1428aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$u,$ $v,$ and $w$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339222} {:answer/id #uuid "bab50867-fb33-5144-a7b7-4401bc9c42cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$u$ and $w$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6afb012e-f43b-5f26-b7e5-bcaddcd5f573" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339220} :answer-field/id #uuid "65bd660f-b589-5a19-b727-967684559e4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79d3c480-8760-5e06-b199-4e6b67572a49" :question/math-academy-id "q-135485" :question/problem "Which of the following are stochastic vectors?\n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 \\\\ 0.7 \\\\ 0.5\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}0.6 \\\\ -0.1 \\\\ 0.5\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0.15 \\\\ 0.95\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our vectors in turn.\n\n- Vector $u$ is *not* a stochastic vector since it contains negative entries.\n\n- Vector $v$ is *not* a stochastic vector since it contains negative entries.\n\n- Vector $w$is *not* a stochastic vector since the entries do not add up to one:\n\n$$\n0.15+0.95=1.1\\ne 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66b24a98-c641-5e08-8bec-30f221d1bb22" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Stochastic Vectors and Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8b4fbc5-a04c-5b22-b4cf-4bc2d56c5a72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339226} {:answer/id #uuid "11bb2216-6b97-5be8-98b3-b5f516c93f4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339227} {:answer/id #uuid "9162a11b-976e-5e4a-892d-9ce8f9899756" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339228} {:answer/id #uuid "279a078b-cb83-5d69-89c6-4b12d0e6cdde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339229} {:answer/id #uuid "786eb244-0f74-5bf7-93f7-0eb7dad83d5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "786eb244-0f74-5bf7-93f7-0eb7dad83d5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339230} :answer-field/id #uuid "40cfa081-d29b-57f7-a9f0-dc54a0338a6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d9e8f97c-57bc-5f59-8108-1c5296ef33a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-135488" :question/problem "Which of the following are stochastic matrices?\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0 & 0.9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0.9 & 0 & 0.1\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 1.1 & 0.8\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$ A matrix is stochastic if all its columns are stochastic vectors.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices in turn.\n\n- Matrix $U$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its second column is not a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries don't add up to one): \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0 & 0.9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0.9 & 0 & 0.1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Matrix $V$  is *not* a stochastic matrix since its first column is not a stochastic vector (it contains negative entries): \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 1.1 & 0.8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Matrix $W$ is a stochastic matrix. Indeed, each column is a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries are non-negative and add up to one): \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.7\\end{aligned}]\\,\\begin{aligned}0.5+0.5=1 \\\\ 0.3+0.7=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$W$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abbdd778-7ace-5539-9d6f-cdf01804b237" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Stochastic Matrix Given Some Data"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "940549cf-d44d-5a59-b03f-3ac2d3b64279" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Ice-creams** | **Cakes** |\n| To: | **Ice-creams** | $0.35$ | $0.42$ |\n| **Cakes** | $0.65$ | $0.58$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339233} {:answer/id #uuid "591279c4-6bcf-5d4b-82b5-3275b1c2211f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Ice-creams** | **Cakes** |\n| To: | **Ice-creams** | $0.65$ | $0.58$ |\n| **Cakes** | $0.35$ | $0.42$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339234} {:answer/id #uuid "b66a82ad-b1b5-52bd-bf3f-503669a2e570" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Ice-creams** | **Cakes** |\n| To: | **Ice-creams** | $0.42$ | $0.65$ |\n| **Cakes** | $0.58$ | $0.35$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339235} {:answer/id #uuid "20660e89-d1b1-516f-85b5-56d5c2571b50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Ice-creams** | **Cakes** |\n| To: | **Ice-creams** | $0.65$ | $0.42$ |\n| **Cakes** | $0.35$ | $0.58$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339236} {:answer/id #uuid "716fff59-4e43-5fa6-9c44-eb178499fff3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Ice-creams** | **Cakes** |\n| To: | **Ice-creams** | $0.42$ | $0.35$ |\n| **Cakes** | $0.58$ | $0.65$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20660e89-d1b1-516f-85b5-56d5c2571b50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Ice-creams** | **Cakes** |\n| To: | **Ice-creams** | $0.65$ | $0.42$ |\n| **Cakes** | $0.35$ | $0.58$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339236} :answer-field/id #uuid "da18d71e-b316-5416-b031-74a8bd55ec89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b2ff55d-f53a-5391-85d9-6aee7280619f" :question/math-academy-id "q-135554" :question/problem "There are two groups of children in a certain city: those who prefer ice cream and those who prefer cakes. A study has shown that, each week\n\n- from the children that prefer ice cream, $35%$ change to cakes, and\n\n- from the children that prefer cakes, $42%$ change to ice cream.\n\nWhich of the following tables describes how these children change their preferences each week?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the following situation for each week: \n\n- $35%$ of children who preferred ice creams swap to cakes. This implies that $65%$ of them continue to prefer ice creams.\n\n- $42%$ of children who preferred cakes swap to ice creams. This implies that $58%$ of them continue to prefer cakes.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Ice-creams** | **Cakes** |\n| To: | **Ice-creams** | $0.65$ | $0.42$ |\n| **Cakes** | $0.35$ | $0.58$ |  |\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0.65 & 0.42 \\\\ 0.35 & 0.58\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abbdd778-7ace-5539-9d6f-cdf01804b237" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Stochastic Matrix Given Some Data"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3c0b6e6-4b95-54fe-82a8-7d935bd53119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Rain** | **Dry** |\n| To: | **Rain** | $0.62$ | $0.45$ |\n| **Dry** | $0.38$ | $0.55$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339240} {:answer/id #uuid "213007d7-02a0-5480-a695-99e3de3a68cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Rain** | **Dry** |\n| To: | **Rain** | $0.38$ | $0.45$ |\n| **Dry** | $0.62$ | $0.55$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339241} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ab16f2-8651-5f58-b192-80bc6e0ee97b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Rain** | **Dry** |\n| To: | **Rain** | $0.45$ | $0.38$ |\n| **Dry** | $0.55$ | $0.62$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339242} {:answer/id #uuid "a7902499-2284-5831-a9a7-7969589eb875" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Rain** | **Dry** |\n| To: | **Rain** | $0.62$ | $0.55$ |\n| **Dry** | $0.38$ | $0.45$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339243} {:answer/id #uuid "25f39168-7656-5388-986f-ec3e14112dd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Rain** | **Dry** |\n| To: | **Rain** | $0.45$ | $0.62$ |\n| **Dry** | $0.55$ | $0.38$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3c0b6e6-4b95-54fe-82a8-7d935bd53119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Rain** | **Dry** |\n| To: | **Rain** | $0.62$ | $0.45$ |\n| **Dry** | $0.38$ | $0.55$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339240} :answer-field/id #uuid "a538507c-4c81-51a5-aafd-942ff5ef973c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "27fa3263-36e3-5305-83f8-d0188906560c" :question/math-academy-id "q-135556" :question/problem "A study shows that in a particular region\n\n- there is a $62%$ chance of another rainy day after a rainy day, and\n\n- there is a $55%$ chance of another dry day after a dry day.\n\nWhich of the following tables describes how the weather changes?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the following situation for each day: \n\n- If it is rainy today, there is a $62%$ chance it will be rainy tomorrow. This implies that if it is rainy today, there is a $38%$ chance that it will be dry tomorrow.\n\n- If it is dry today, there is a $55%$ chance that it will be dry tomorrow. This implies that if it is dry today, there is a $45%$ chance that it will be rainy tomorrow\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Rain** | **Dry** |\n| To: | **Rain** | $0.62$ | $0.45$ |\n| **Dry** | $0.38$ | $0.55$ |  |\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0.62 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.38 & 0.55\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abbdd778-7ace-5539-9d6f-cdf01804b237" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Stochastic Matrix Given Some Data"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0e1c674-7298-5cf6-b1c4-7864248e31b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339247} {:answer/id #uuid "ee7cb24b-3fa3-5d91-8915-efbde55bcc63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339248} {:answer/id #uuid "ee00f827-07ae-5f2b-989e-c07f5addf3d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339249} {:answer/id #uuid "78376574-492b-5e5f-a57f-809c997a9bd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339250} {:answer/id #uuid "9fd55427-5528-5be3-893e-c5c17cfcb5b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee7cb24b-3fa3-5d91-8915-efbde55bcc63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339248} :answer-field/id #uuid "794c005a-3524-518c-807a-62738cd42c88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e83e16e-b8fd-55a5-9aa6-130410640d20" :question/math-academy-id "q-135565" :question/problem "A study shows that for a particular area in a specific season,\n\n- on a day that follows a day without precipitation, there is a $25%$ chance to have rain and a $35%$ chance to have snow.\n\n- on a day that follows a rainy day, there is a $40%$ chance not to have precipitation and a $5%$ chance to have snow.\n\n- on a day that follows a snowy day, there is a $50%$ chance not to have precipitation and a $5%$ chance to have rain.\n\nThe stochastic matrix that describes this situation is given below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}a & 0.4 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.25 & b & 0.05 \\\\ 0.35 & 0.05 & c\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the following situation for this area:\n\n- On a day that follows a day without precipitation, there is a $25%$ chance to have rain and a $35%$ chance to have snow. This implies a $40%$ chance of not having precipitation on this day.\n\n- On a day that follows a rainy day, there is a $40%$ chance not to have precipitation and a $5%$ chance to have snow. This implies a $55%$ chance of having rain on this day.\n\n- On a day that follows a snowy day, there is a $50%$ chance not to have precipitation and a $5%$ chance to have rain. This implies a $45%$ chance of having snow on this day.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **No Precipitation** | **Rain** | **Snow** |\n| To: | **No Precipitation** | $0.4$ | $0.4$ | $0.5$ |\n| **Rain** | $0.25$ | $0.55$ | $0.05$ |  |\n| **Snow** | $0.35$ | $0.05$ | $0.45$ |  |\n\nWe can write the corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.4 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.25 & 0.55 & 0.05 \\\\ 0.35 & 0.05 & 0.45\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0.4+0.55+0.45=1.4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abbdd778-7ace-5539-9d6f-cdf01804b237" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Stochastic Matrix Given Some Data"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7bf1303-5570-5700-9fd6-556fc2eef36a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339254} {:answer/id #uuid "3bc0e126-c04b-54a2-8ff4-bacf99c7f908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339255} {:answer/id #uuid "cc5160a5-9c20-5ada-89be-2916efdec933" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339256} {:answer/id #uuid "f204d38d-30bf-589a-be26-2341da76de50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339257} {:answer/id #uuid "2dd3e102-bfe1-5ed3-9404-9d359d8d8ee5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f204d38d-30bf-589a-be26-2341da76de50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339257} :answer-field/id #uuid "70dae69e-1f08-56a4-9010-50d401036fd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e269fcad-8675-50e2-b907-1b09ce78f588" :question/math-academy-id "q-135566" :question/problem "There are three cable TV providers in a particular apartment block: provider $A,$ provider $B,$ and provider $C.$ A study has shown that, each year\n\n- from the tenants using provider $A,$ $80%$ swap to provider $B$ and $10%$ swap to provider $C,$\n\n- from the tenants using provider $B,$ $25%$ swap to provider $A$ and $25%$ swap to provider $C,$\n\n- from the tenants using provider $C,$ $70%$ swap to provider $A$ and $10%$ swap to provider $B.$\n\nThe stochastic matrix that describes this situation is given below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & a & 0.7 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.5 & b \\\\ c & 0.25 & 0.2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the following situation for each year:\n\n- From the tenants using provider $A,$ $80%$ swap to provider $B$ and $10%$ swap to provider $C.$  This implies that $10%$ of them continue using provider $A.$\n\n- From the tenants using provider $B,$ $25%$ swap to provider $A$ and $25%$ swap to provider $C.$ This implies that $50%$ of them continue using provider $B.$\n\n- From the tenants using provider $C,$ $70%$ swap to provider $A$ and $10%$ swap to provider $B.$ This implies that $20%$ of them continue using provider $C.$\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Provider A** | **Provider B** | **Provider C** |\n| To: | **Provider A** | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.7$ |\n| **Provider B** | $0.8$ | $0.5$ | $0.1$ |  |\n| **Provider C** | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.2$ |  |\n\nWe can write the corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0.25 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.5 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.25 & 0.2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0.25+0.1+0.1=0.45.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4821de45-1b6b-5b4f-be0e-96cf5395bc8d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a State Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be445be2-e50f-5cae-92ce-28fb012212d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.61 \\\\ 0.39\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339261} {:answer/id #uuid "0e6bfa1d-7699-5ee9-8a24-c74008b47c61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.516 \\\\ 0.484\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339262} {:answer/id #uuid "d55dcf76-b67d-53c1-a18d-364515a5500a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.39 \\\\ 0.61\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339263} {:answer/id #uuid "fde4a904-fed1-58c7-967e-990743830cd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.484 \\\\ 0.516\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339264} {:answer/id #uuid "e47bbafa-fdd1-50d4-838c-eeed8b55f5f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.844 \\\\ 0.156\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fde4a904-fed1-58c7-967e-990743830cd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.484 \\\\ 0.516\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339264} :answer-field/id #uuid "f18b001c-3561-5a01-ad1b-840a72b9f3e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b15112f4-7893-524a-8de9-46c29499aff4" :question/math-academy-id "q-35789" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.25 & 0.85 \\\\ 0.75 & 0.15\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 \\\\ 0.6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector ${x}_{0}$ given above. Calculate the state vector ${x}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the state vector ${x}_{2},$ we apply two steps of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0},\\,{x}_{2}=P{x}_{1}\n$$\n\nAfter the first step, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.25 & 0.85 \\\\ 0.75 & 0.15\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.4 \\\\ 0.6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.61 \\\\ 0.39\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, after the second step, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.25 & 0.85 \\\\ 0.75 & 0.15\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.61 \\\\ 0.39\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.484 \\\\ 0.516\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339266 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abbdd778-7ace-5539-9d6f-cdf01804b237" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Stochastic Matrix Given Some Data"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af2c63a0-0d32-5df7-a3af-d413f0136b33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Healthy** | **Sick** |\n| To: | **Healthy** | $0.38$ | $0.45$ |\n| **Sick** | $0.62$ | $0.55$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339268} {:answer/id #uuid "6f41ce78-bac1-5469-97b1-d4849b43ec7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Healthy** | **Sick** |\n| To: | **Healthy** | $0.62$ | $0.45$ |\n| **Sick** | $0.38$ | $0.55$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339269} {:answer/id #uuid "730345ce-19aa-576d-b4f4-876fdbe59cb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Healthy** | **Sick** |\n| To: | **Healthy** | $0.45$ | $0.62$ |\n| **Sick** | $0.55$ | $0.38$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339270} {:answer/id #uuid "c6296c7d-b32e-5926-8df8-d009908ac0e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Healthy** | **Sick** |\n| To: | **Healthy** | $0.62$ | $0.55$ |\n| **Sick** | $0.38$ | $0.45$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339271} {:answer/id #uuid "a6adbf4c-76d5-5f42-ad73-395517c9758c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Healthy** | **Sick** |\n| To: | **Healthy** | $0.45$ | $0.38$ |\n| **Sick** | $0.55$ | $0.62$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f41ce78-bac1-5469-97b1-d4849b43ec7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Healthy** | **Sick** |\n| To: | **Healthy** | $0.62$ | $0.45$ |\n| **Sick** | $0.38$ | $0.55$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339269} :answer-field/id #uuid "008ae76b-f244-557a-9166-b833ae29fc89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "96e846e9-ab89-5268-ba2a-aaad5210df91" :question/math-academy-id "q-35843" :question/problem "In a particular country, there are two groups of people: those who are healthy and those who are sick. A study has shown that each quarter\n\n- $38%$ of the healthy people tend to get sick, and\n\n- $45%$ of the sick people tend to improve their health.\n\nWhich of the following tables describes how people's health status changes?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the following situation for each semester: \n\n- $38%$ of the healthy people tend to get sick. This implies that $62%$ of them remain healthy.\n\n- $45%$ of the sick people tend to improve their health. This implies that $55%$ of them remain sick.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Healthy** | **Sick** |\n| To: | **Healthy** | $0.62$ | $0.45$ |\n| **Sick** | $0.38$ | $0.55$ |  |\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0.62 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.38 & 0.55\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124840 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e93dde4-a8e8-548a-801e-3d9a320a4513" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Stochastic Matrix and Making a Prediction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b76f2582-fea2-5626-98ba-82d34f627a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "480000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122063} {:answer/id #uuid "cfba4be1-9c4a-5c04-be11-f069e3cf46f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "400000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122064} {:answer/id #uuid "dd42c5e0-7138-583a-a92f-19729c14db8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "640000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122065} {:answer/id #uuid "eeec030c-7b86-58d4-b092-fe58988f8a90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "430000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122066} {:answer/id #uuid "2bc2e0e9-2a0a-5c8c-8ab7-51db96111980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "520000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b76f2582-fea2-5626-98ba-82d34f627a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "480000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122063} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb82541c-1e5d-58a7-bb27-5fed853fe87b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122062}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62389cec-4c75-591c-8976-ec4ed362e628" :question/math-academy-id "q-35845" :question/problem "A certain company produces two types of smartphones: Phone $A$ and Phone $B.$ The following table describes how the number of customers changes between both types of smartphones each year:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Phone $A$** | **Phone $B$** |\n| To: | **Phone $A$** | $0.6$ | $0.3$ |\n| **Phone $B$** | $0.4$ | $0.7$ |  |\n\nThat is, $40%$ of Phone $A$ customers switch to Phone $B$ each year, and $30%$ of Phone $B$ customers switch to Phone $A.$\n\nCurrently, $600\\,000$ customers use Phone $A,$ while $400\\,000$ customers use Phone $B.$ How many customers will use Phone $A$ in one year? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.6 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each smartphone is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}600\\,000 \\\\ 400\\,000\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}600\\,000 \\\\ 400\\,000\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.6 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.6 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.6 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.48 \\\\ 0.52\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one year, $48%$ of customers will use Phone $A.$ Computing $48%$ of $1\\,000\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.48=480\\,000.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $480\\,000$ customers will use Phone $A$ in one year."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4821de45-1b6b-5b4f-be0e-96cf5395bc8d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a State Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5825d08-86a3-50d7-8984-8f2cd8d0faf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0.393 \\\\ 0.607\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122069} {:answer/id #uuid "f9ad3b9e-20a5-5b74-9a37-9df7886cff6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0.413 \\\\ 0.587\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122070} {:answer/id #uuid "2f7fb7ea-e2b3-5992-9bf6-e41c09e0cbb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0.611 \\\\ 0.389\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122071} {:answer/id #uuid "491996ac-bf8d-5eee-a987-0588261d1f8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0.442 \\\\ 0.558\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122072} {:answer/id #uuid "7b9686e5-79d6-5616-8acb-0e5b13b9ae3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0.526 \\\\ 0.474\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9ad3b9e-20a5-5b74-9a37-9df7886cff6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0.413 \\\\ 0.587\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122070} :answer-field/id #uuid "98df5681-9ccd-5b69-82eb-39e17040c2c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f3da8136-058f-5b0b-abbd-f2c6d4a902e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-35846" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.5\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector ${x}_{0}$ given above. Calculate the state vector ${x}_{2}.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "To find the state vector ${x}_{2},$ we apply two steps of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0},\\,{x}_{2}=P{x}_{1}\n$$\n\nAfter the first step, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.29 \\\\ 0.71\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, after the second step, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.29 \\\\ 0.71\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.413 \\\\ 0.587\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
